Study: security and exposure evidence
Mode: read-only source/config/public GET inspection; no exploit or mutation

Observed source/config:
- gateway /opt/v3-cursor-api/gateway/app/backend/main.py:163-177 defines internal/user checks; missing Authorization falls back to anonymous user.
- main.py:237-363 exposes /api/render/{tc} without a user dependency.
- main.py:868-960 exposes upload, job-list, job-control and outputs routes without a user dependency.
- main.py:962 onward exposes download route without a user dependency.
- public OpenAPI had no global security scheme and no selected route security declaration.

Observed public metadata:
- unauthenticated health/cluster responses exposed worker IDs, internal URLs, encoder capabilities, health state and system metrics.
- outputs endpoint exposed job/file metadata and returned 100 records.

Host perimeter:
- ufw was not installed
- nftables INPUT policy: accept
- iptables INPUT/FORWARD/OUTPUT policies: ACCEPT
- many listeners bound to 0.0.0.0/[::] on the co-hosted server

Headers and file probes:
- tested responses did not show HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options or Referrer-Policy headers
- /.git/config, /.env, /old/.git/config and /old/.env returned UI fallback in current direct checks; no raw secret file was confirmed
- detached legacy log contained historical scanner requests, which is operational evidence, not proof of current raw-file exposure

Classification: critical findings requiring remediation before production acceptance. No exploit was attempted and no secret was copied into this artifact.
